-- Passage rg4-story-details-inference-p04
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('1dd4055c-49cd-505d-9769-6ba5e775b6d2', 'rg4-story-details-inference-p04', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g4-story-details-inference'), null,
        '3-5', $q$Wood for the Stove$q$, $q$In the winter of 1885, the schoolhouse in Clover Flats had only one room and one iron stove. Each week, a different student was in charge of the fire. This week it was Ada Lindqvist's turn.

Ada had to arrive before anyone else. She left her farm while the stars were still out and walked two miles through the snow. Her lunch pail was tied to her belt so her hands were free to carry kindling.

When she reached the schoolhouse, the woodbox was nearly empty. Someone had used most of the logs on Friday and had not refilled it. Ada frowned. Then she went out to the woodpile behind the building and brushed the snow off the top logs.

She carried six armloads inside. Her mittens grew wet, and her fingers ached. By the time she struck the match, the windows were gray with morning light.

At eight o'clock, the other students stamped in, red-cheeked and stiff. They crowded around the stove and held out their hands. Little Samuel Ortiz, who was only six, rubbed his cold hands together and laughed.

Miss Hale, the teacher, looked at the full woodbox. Then she looked at Ada's wet mittens drying on the stove rail. She said nothing, but at recess she let Ada ring the big brass bell.$q$, 'draft')
