insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('e5f0d6de-4aff-5438-ac34-bbf9bf5d8350', 'rg5-argument-evidence-p02', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g5-argument-evidence'), null,
        '3-5', $q$Bats Are Good Neighbors$q$, $q$Many people feel nervous when they see a bat swooping through the evening sky. But bats are helpful animals, and towns should protect the places where they live.

Bats help farmers. A single little brown bat can catch hundreds of insects in one hour. Many of those insects, such as moths and beetles, eat crops like corn and cotton. When bats eat these pests, farmers do not need to spray as many chemicals on their fields.

Bats also help plants grow. In warm places, some bats drink nectar from flowers at night. As they move from flower to flower, they carry pollen. Plants such as wild bananas and agave depend on bats to make seeds.

Bats even help scientists. Researchers study how bats find food in total darkness. Bats make high sounds and listen for echoes bouncing back from objects. Scientists have used this idea to design tools that help ships find objects underwater.

Sadly, many groups of bats are shrinking. Old trees and caves where bats rest are being cleared away. Towns can help by leaving dead trees standing in parks when it is safe and by putting up bat houses.

Bats may look strange, but they work hard for us every night. They deserve our protection.$q$, 'draft')
