-- Passage rg5-argument-evidence-p03
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('0a33b963-ea79-57ea-89ec-4571e3a94306', 'rg5-argument-evidence-p03', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g5-argument-evidence'), null,
        '3-5', $q$Every Student Should Learn to Type$q$, $q$Students today write many assignments on computers. Yet many of them still type with two fingers, hunting for each key. Schools should teach keyboarding to every student by fifth grade.

Typing well saves time. A student who hunts for keys might type about ten words per minute. A student who has practiced with all ten fingers can often type thirty words per minute or more. That means a one-page report could take a third of the time.

Typing well also helps students write better. When fingers move quickly, the mind can focus on ideas instead of on finding letters. At Linden Park School, fifth graders took a twelve-week typing course. Afterward, their teachers noticed that students wrote longer stories with more details.

Typing is a skill students will use for the rest of their lives. Adults type emails, forms and reports at many jobs. Learning the right way early prevents bad habits that are hard to break later.

Some people argue that students can learn to type on their own at home. But not every family has a computer, and students who teach themselves often keep the two-finger habit. Teaching it at school makes sure everyone gets a fair chance.

A few short lessons each week would give students a skill that lasts a lifetime.$q$, 'draft')
