-- Passage rg5-argument-evidence-p05
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('a5e8df0e-db30-546a-a37c-79fc3d774e8b', 'rg5-argument-evidence-p05', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g5-argument-evidence'), null,
        '3-5', $q$Try More Than One Sport$q$, $q$Some young athletes pick one sport by age eight and play it all year long. They join fall teams, winter clinics and summer camps for the same game. Many coaches who work with children suggest a different path. Kids should play several sports before choosing just one.

Playing different sports builds a wider set of skills. Basketball teaches quick footwork. Swimming builds strong lungs. Gymnastics improves balance. A soccer player who also swims and plays basketball brings all of those strengths onto the field.

Playing several sports also protects young bodies. When a child repeats the same motions every day, the same muscles and joints get tired again and again. A study of 1,200 young athletes found that those who played only one sport all year were about twice as likely to get overuse injuries, such as sore elbows or knees.

Trying different sports keeps the game fun. Many children who play one sport all year say they feel bored or stressed. In one youth league survey, 7 out of 10 players who quit before age thirteen said the sport had stopped being fun.

Finally, kids may find a sport they love that they never expected. Rosa Delgado played soccer for years before trying volleyball at a summer camp. Volleyball quickly became her favorite.

There is plenty of time to focus on one sport later. Childhood is the time to explore.$q$, 'draft')
