-- Passage rg5-authors-purpose-p04
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('0eae53fe-2dd6-5434-902c-57134bce1b05', 'rg5-authors-purpose-p04', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g5-authors-purpose'), null,
        '3-5', $q$A Frog No One Had Named$q$, $q$Account 1: From a Science Magazine for Kids

Scientists have discovered a new kind of frog in a misty mountain valley. The frog is about as long as a paper clip and has bright green skin with gold spots. Its call sounds like a tiny bell ringing. The team named it the valley bell frog. Scientists say there are still many unnamed animals in forests like this one.

Account 2: From a Talk by Ines Quispe, Local Guide

I grew up in this valley, and my grandmother taught me to listen for this frog after rain. We call it the little bell because of its ringing call. My family has known it for as long as anyone can remember. I am happy that scientists came to study it. But I smile when I read that it was "discovered." Our valley knew it first.

Account 3: From a Report by Dr. Henrik Sato, Scientist

Naming a new animal takes careful work. First, our team recorded the frog's bell-like call and compared it to the calls of known frogs. Next, we measured dozens of frogs and studied their colors. Then a lab compared tiny samples of their cells with those of similar frogs. It took us two years to be sure this frog was different from every frog on record. Local guides like Ines Quispe showed us exactly where to look.$q$, 'draft')
