-- Passage rg5-character-setting-plot-p07
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('990273d6-2c1e-54fc-a5d9-07364f7585d2', 'rg5-character-setting-plot-p07', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g5-character-setting-plot'), null,
        '3-5', $q$The Bridge Test$q$, $q$Ms. Rivera's fifth graders had one week to build bridges from craft sticks and glue. Each bridge had to stretch across a gap between two desks. On Friday, the class would stack books on them to see which held the most.

Hana Sato and Luis Ortega worked as partners. Luis wanted their bridge to look amazing. He painted it blue and added a tall arch on top. Hana wanted to glue extra triangles along the sides, but Luis said they would look messy.

On the first test day, they set their bridge across the gap. Ms. Rivera placed one book, then two. On the third book, the bridge sagged in the middle. On the fourth, it snapped with a loud crack. The tall arch had added weight but no strength.

Luis stared at the broken pieces. Hana picked up the arch. "It looked great," she said. "But looks don't hold books."

Ms. Rivera gave them one extra day to rebuild. This time, Luis listened. They skipped the paint and the arch. Instead, they glued rows of small triangles along both sides, just as Hana had sketched.

On Friday, their plain brown bridge held eleven books before it bent. Luis grinned and pointed at the triangles. "Next time," he said, "we start with your sketch."$q$, 'draft')
