-- Passage rg5-connections-p05
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('194c1d09-de29-5a7e-8b4a-d9ad42c5bbbf', 'rg5-connections-p05', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g5-connections'), null,
        '3-5', $q$Passing the Baton$q$, $q$In a relay race, four runners share one race. Each runner carries a short tube called a baton for part of the distance and then hands it to the next runner. The team's time depends on the speed of every runner and on how well they pass the baton.

The pass must happen inside a marked area called the exchange zone. If the baton changes hands outside the zone, the team is disqualified. This rule makes timing very important.

Coach Lena Petrov teaches the team a careful system. As the incoming runner gets close, the next runner starts running before the baton arrives. That way, the two are moving at almost the same speed when they meet. The incoming runner calls out a signal, and the next runner reaches back without turning around. The baton is placed firmly into the open hand.

If the next runner starts too early, the incoming runner cannot catch up inside the zone. If the next runner starts too late, the incoming runner has to slow down, and the team loses time.

Last season, Coach Petrov's team did not have the fastest runners in the league. Still, they won the championship. Their smooth passes saved small fractions of a second at every exchange.$q$, 'draft')
