insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('3fb66a5c-c9de-55c8-a1b1-0fad14ff475e', 'rg5-context-clues-p01', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g5-context-clues'), null,
        '3-5', $q$Rivers of Ice$q$, $q$A glacier is a huge mass of ice that forms over many years. In very cold places, snow falls faster than it can melt. The snow piles up, layer after layer. The weight of the top layers presses down on the snow below until it becomes dense, or tightly packed, ice.

Glaciers may look still, but they are always moving. Their own heavy weight makes them slide downhill. Most glaciers creep along so slowly that you cannot see them move. A few, however, are rapid. They can travel many feet in a single day.

As a glacier moves, it scrapes the ground beneath it. It picks up rocks, pebbles, and sand. These materials are called debris, which simply means loose bits of broken stuff. When the glacier melts, it leaves the debris behind in long piles.

Today many glaciers are shrinking. Warmer summers cause more ice to melt than new snow can replace. Because of this, some glaciers have retreated, pulling back up the valleys they once filled. Scientists keep track of these changes by placing markers in the ice and checking them each year.$q$, 'draft')
